{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied PostgreSQL schema as branch_stock on node1 and preserve its supplied data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"branch_stock was created from stock.sql. Final post-restart evidence shows both tables with the four supplied records and an unchanged source-file SHA-256 checksum.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c2dd8465fae419d","cmd-bf4ca780b81f4c5f","seeded-state-node1"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow stock_app from node2 to read and update stock quantities, while preventing supplier_costs access and schema changes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Remote node2 tests directly demonstrate SELECT and transactional UPDATE on stock, denial of supplier_costs reads and CREATE, and rollback to the original quantities. These behaviors persisted after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4a312bfe92ab4874","cmd-4f30b4a79c9d407c","cmd-da90e207ea734e69","cmd-170e562f657044b9"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Give auditor read-only access to stock and supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The auditor remotely read every supplied row from both tables, while UPDATE and schema creation were denied. Final ACLs grant only SELECT, and post-restart behavior confirms continued read-only access.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ecfe5d1ed2cc49cb","cmd-da90e207ea734e69","cmd-170e562f657044b9"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Establish separate credentials for stock_app and auditor.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Distinct password files were generated for the two login roles, copied to node2, and protected as root-owned mode 0600 files inside a mode 0700 directory. Both credentials independently authenticated after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1c2dd8465fae419d","cmd-05a76351c622452d","cmd-da90e207ea734e69","cmd-170e562f657044b9"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Preserve the supplied records under /srv/legacy2/database-reader-writer-roles.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The supplied stock.sql checksum remained identical to the preparation baseline, and final database contents match its two stock and two supplier-cost records. No executor command modified the supplied source file.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-10a106dcfda54f98","cmd-bf4ca780b81f4c5f"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use legacy-platform-compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 7.10 and kernel 2.6.22-14-generic were retained. PostgreSQL 8.2.11 packages came from the configured Ubuntu Gutsy repositories, and final service/client checks succeeded after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ff48a05e81094e38","cmd-3e42e18265b14693","cmd-112dbd7e7fb8453b","cmd-170e562f657044b9","cmd-da90e207ea734e69","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.66,"summary":"Functionality is clean, package state remained consistent, and temporary database mutations were rolled back. However, several unnecessary retained mutations reduce hygiene: cmd-1cc9a5538f3b4d99 added a root SSH authorization and cmd-a9b9801292e04a0e wrote an SSH private key for a transfer attempt that failed, with no removal captured; cmd-b81c77b9475b421d and cmd-5e80f22509a746be created the successful fallback root RSA key/authorization, but retaining that passwordless root path after transfer was unnecessary; cmd-1569e7e78df849b2 created configuration backup files with no cleanup evidence; and cmd-da90e207ea734e69 left two denial-test files in /tmp, confirmed in the final lifecycle snapshot. These were retained rather than restored. Required package installation, bounded rollback tests, restart tests, and the corrected PostgreSQL listener configuration were causally related to the task and are not penalized.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1cc9a5538f3b4d99","cmd-a9b9801292e04a0e","cmd-b81c77b9475b421d","cmd-5e80f22509a746be","cmd-1569e7e78df849b2","cmd-da90e207ea734e69","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, data, credentials, and privilege behavior. An initial node1 reboot exposed a listener-startup problem, but the executor corrected it and demonstrated a second complete successful restart transition. Operational hygiene is reduced by retained SSH transfer credentials/authorizations, apparent configuration backups, and two confirmed temporary test files.","limitations":["The final node2 probes recaptured its kernel and working legacy PostgreSQL client but not the Ubuntu release string; unchanged platform identity and the package transaction showing only three client packages installed provide supporting evidence.","The lifecycle listening-socket collector returned an empty inventory even though direct final netstat and service checks showed PostgreSQL listening; the direct task-specific observations were used.","Final lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /root/.ssh or PostgreSQL configuration backups, so their retention is inferred from their creation and the absence of any cleanup command in the complete timeline."]}
